Despite notable advancements in rotorcraft aerodynamics, several critical gaps persist in current research. A primary limitation is the absence of mid-fidelity aerodynamic models specifically optimized for reduced-order model (ROM) extraction. While high-fidelity simulations and comprehensive CFD tools exist, they are rarely designed with the computational and structural constraints necessary for ROM generation, thereby limiting their deployment in real-time applications such as flight simulation and control design [4, 7, 12]. Moreover, a substantial portion of existing CFD-based studies particularly those addressing complex phenomena such as vortex ring state (VRS) and loss of tail rotor effectiveness (LTE) lack rigorous experimental validation, diminishing their predictive reliability for safety-critical scenarios [3, 4, 8].
In addition, there remains a dearth of real-time capable aerodynamic models that can accurately capture the nonlinear, unsteady flow dynamics inherent in rotorcraft operations. Current ROMs are often overly simplified or insufficiently robust to generalize across the wide range of operational regimes, including transitions into and out of VRS, tail rotor failures, and ground effect conditions [7, 10, 11]. This oversimplification undermines their utility in high-fidelity flight training and performance prediction environments. Furthermore, much of the existing research exhibits a fragmented methodology, wherein computational and experimental approaches are pursued in isolation. The lack of an integrated simulation experiment ROM workflow reduces the reliability and transferability of the resulting models.
